> **Dinesh:** There was a paper a (long!) while back where some MITer used a liquid gate for pseudocolor. The idea was to place the film in a liquid gate (a closed enclosure filled with a particular liquid) with plain water. Then she made an exposure, drained the liquid gate, filled it with TEA and made another exposure. She continued this process for two or three exposures with different concentrations of TEA to get a range of colors. If you're interested, I'll try and find the paper, but I believe it was sometime in 87 or 88. I've also seen this technique around about 82. Some researher at CalTech was using liquid gates to identify the bones of small animals. He immersed the film in water and shot the bone, then changed the liquid in the gate to alcohol and shot the same bone again. The change in the index of the film surround gave enough of a phase difference to cause interference lines on the bone. The interference lines gave information on the small details that differentiated the bones of one small animal from another.

Some attempts with liquid systems I am ware of:

Around the early 80s Newport used to sell a film holder (very expensive of course!), involving a water tank;

then there was a paper by Sainov et al, Color Reflection Holography, SPIE 615, 1986. That group used sodium sulfate solutions as the immersion liquid;

and of course the Walker/Benton system based on alcohol/water + enzyme treatment (see Kaveh's post);

I think Helge Nareid (University of Aberdeen ?) is doing underwater holograms...
